# mackas.conf -- configuration for mackas
#
# Copyright (C) 2026 Koen Kooi <koen@dominion.thruhere.net>
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
#
# Copy to one of the searched locations and edit. First match wins:
#
#   $MACKAS_CONF                  (explicit, via env var)
#   ~/.config/mackas/config       (per-user)
#   ~/.mackas.conf                (per-user, dotfile)
#
# Or point at one directly:  mackas --config /path/to/conf
#
# This file is SOURCED AS SHELL. You can compute values, but anything you
# execute here runs with your privileges -- keep it to assignments.
#
# ./mackas.conf IS NOT SEARCHED, and that is deliberate.
#
# It used to be, and because this file is shell, that made merely cd'ing into
# a directory dangerous: unpack a tarball or clone a repo that happens to
# contain a mackas.conf, run any mackas command inside it -- even
# `mackas help` -- and that file ran as you. A cwd-relative config cannot be
# made safe, because the whole point of one is that the directory is not
# necessarily yours.
#
# A per-project config still works. It just has to be asked for out loud:
#
#   mackas --config ./mackas.conf status
#
# For the same reason, the two searched paths above are skipped (with a
# warning saying so) unless you or root own the file AND its directory, and
# neither is writable by group or other. If mackas says it is ignoring your
# config, this is what it wants:
#
#   chmod go-w ~/.mackas.conf ~
#
# Values are checked before they are used: any setting mackas interpolates
# into the env.sh or kas fragment it generates may not contain a double quote,
# a backtick, or a control character (a newline especially) -- those corrupt
# the generated file rather than appearing in it. Spaces, '$' and apostrophes
# are all fine; a MACKAS_ROOT with a space in it (a volume named "My Disk")
# is fully supported.
#
# PRECEDENCE, lowest to highest:
#
#   built-in defaults  ->  this file  ->  environment  ->  command-line flags
#
# So an env var always beats this file:
#
#   MACKAS_MEMORY=48g mackas setup
#
# ...and --set beats even that:
#
#   mackas --set MACKAS_MEMORY=48g setup
#
# Every setting below is also an environment variable of the same name.
# Uncomment only what you want to change; the defaults shown are what the
# script uses if you say nothing. Run `mackas status` to see
# the values actually in effect.


# --- Where the build lives ---------------------------------------------------

# Top-level build directory. REQUIRED -- there is no default, on purpose.
#
# It must be on a CASE-SENSITIVE filesystem: OpenEmbedded breaks in baffling
# ways on case-insensitive APFS/HFS+, and a stock Mac's boot volume (so your
# home directory) is case-insensitive. There is therefore no generic default
# worth shipping -- a $HOME path would pass `check` right up to the
# case-sensitivity probe and then fail there. `check` probes this empirically
# (it creates files; it does not trust diskutil).
#
# Make a case-sensitive volume once, then point MACKAS_ROOT at a dir on it:
#   diskutil apfs addVolume <disk> "Case-sensitive APFS" oe   # see: diskutil list
# or a case-sensitive sparse image that lives as one file on any volume:
#   hdiutil create -type SPARSE -fs "Case-sensitive APFS" -size 200g \
#       -volname oe ~/oe.sparseimage
#   hdiutil attach ~/oe.sparseimage
#
#MACKAS_ROOT="/Volumes/<your-case-sensitive-volume>/oe"

# A short, space-free symlink to MACKAS_ROOT. Everything the build touches goes
# through this path instead of MACKAS_ROOT directly, which buys two things:
#
#   1. No spaces. If your volume name has any (many do), a long tail of OE
#      recipes and third-party build scripts mishandle spaces in paths.
#   2. Short paths. macOS caps AF_UNIX socket paths (sockaddr_un.sun_path)
#      at ~104 characters; bitbake puts ${TOPDIR}/bitbake.sock there. This
#      is kas issue #38. Under Apple container the bind actually happens
#      inside the Linux guest at /build/bitbake.sock so the limit rarely
#      bites -- but keeping host paths short costs nothing and removes the
#      whole class of problem if you ever run bitbake natively.
#
# Default is $HOME/oe rather than /opt/oe: /opt is drwxr-xr-x root:wheel, so
# /opt/oe needs sudo, and $HOME/oe needs none. Both are far under 104 chars.
# Set /opt/oe if you want the extra 6 characters; setup will offer the sudo.
#
#MACKAS_SHORT_LINK="$HOME/oe"

# setup REFUSES a case-insensitive work/ by default (OpenEmbedded corrupts on
# one; the layer checkouts under work/ are a host bind-mount, not an ext4
# volume) -- after first offering to create a case-sensitive workspace image
# mounted at work/ (see MACKAS_WORKSPACE_SIZE below). Set to 0 only if you
# know the case-sensitive parts genuinely live elsewhere.
#MACKAS_REQUIRE_CASE_SENSITIVE=1

# Size of the case-sensitive APFS sparse image setup offers to create at
# work/ when the root turns out to be case-insensitive. Sparse, so this is a
# ceiling, not real disk cost until written -- generous by default since it
# only holds git checkouts (oe-core, bitbake, meta-*, the project layer),
# nothing like TMPDIR/sstate.
#MACKAS_WORKSPACE_SIZE="40G"

# Path of that workspace image, once one exists. Empty by default, and you do
# not normally set this by hand: setup WRITES it here the moment it creates or
# reattaches an image, because it is a record of fact, not a preference.
#
# It has to be recorded because `hdiutil attach` does not survive a reboot: on
# a fresh boot work/ is an ordinary empty directory on the case-insensitive
# drive, indistinguishable from a root that never had an image. With this set,
# every command that touches work/ reattaches the image first -- or refuses to
# run if it cannot. Unset it (mackas unset MACKAS_WORKSPACE_IMAGE) only when
# you have genuinely moved the checkouts off the image.
#MACKAS_WORKSPACE_IMAGE=""


# --- Apple container resources -----------------------------------------------

# Apple container defaults to cpus=4 and memory=1gb PER CONTAINER, which is
# nowhere near enough for bitbake. container 1.1.0 has no
# `container system property set`, so these must be passed per-run; the script
# does that on kas-container's --runtime-args flag.
#
# NOT via KAS_EXTRA_RUNTIME_ARGS: that is not an environment variable at all.
# kas-container blanks it before parsing its arguments, so exporting it is
# silently ignored and you get cpus=4/memory=1gb no matter what you set here.
#
# Default MACKAS_CPUS = physical cores - 2 (min 2), leaving the host usable.
# Quirk: nproc INSIDE the container reports one MORE than the -c value.
#MACKAS_CPUS=18

# Default MACKAS_MEMORY = two thirds of host RAM. The OE guest is a VM, so
# overcommitting here makes the host swap.
#MACKAS_MEMORY="42g"


# --- The ext4 volumes --------------------------------------------------------

# `container volume create -s SIZE NAME` makes a SPARSE ext4 image at
#   ~/Library/Application Support/com.apple.container/volumes/NAME/volume.img
# Sparse means a 120G volume costs ~1.2M until you fill it. It gives you real
# Linux filesystem semantics (hardlinks, xattrs, rename()) inside the
# container -- which an APFS directory reaching the guest over virtiofs cannot,
# and which OpenEmbedded requires.
#
# A FIXED size is the point, not a limitation: the target SSD is an active
# Time Machine destination, and capped images mean a runaway build cannot eat
# the free space that backups need.
#
# There are THREE volumes, not one, named off the stem below:
#
#   ${MACKAS_VOLUME_NAME}-tmp      -> /build      TMPDIR
#   ${MACKAS_VOLUME_NAME}-dl       -> /downloads  DL_DIR
#   ${MACKAS_VOLUME_NAME}-sstate   -> /sstate     SSTATE_DIR
#
# Separate volumes so `mackas clean` can throw TMPDIR away -- the big, churny,
# rebuildable one -- and keep the two caches that are expensive to refill. One
# volume could not do that. They are mounted by passing -v/-e to kas on
# --runtime-args; see "The ext4 volumes" in docs/architecture.md for why they
# cannot be passed as KAS_BUILD_DIR/DL_DIR/SSTATE_DIR instead.
#
# The stem must not contain whitespace: kas-container word-splits
# --runtime-args, so a space would split one mount into two arguments. setup
# refuses a name with one rather than mounting something absurd.
#MACKAS_VOLUME_NAME="oe-build"

# The three caps. They total 200G, which is what the SSD can spare alongside
# Time Machine (it has ~296 GiB free). Sparse, so this is a ceiling, not a
# cost. A volume CANNOT be grown after creation -- container 1.1.0 has no grow
# command -- so getting one badly wrong means `destroy` and start over, which
# is the other reason TMPDIR is not in the same volume as the caches.
#MACKAS_VOLUME_SIZE_TMP="120G"
#MACKAS_VOLUME_SIZE_DL="40G"
#MACKAS_VOLUME_SIZE_SSTATE="40G"

# container 1.1.0 has no config option for the volume storage root, so the
# only way to keep volume.img off the internal disk is to symlink the volumes
# directory. 1 = do that (onto $MACKAS_ROOT/container-volumes).
#MACKAS_RELOCATE_VOLUMES=1


# --- kas / image -------------------------------------------------------------

#KAS_IMAGE="ghcr.io/siemens/kas/kas:5.4"


# --- Project (which layers to build) -----------------------------------------
#
# mackas builds whatever git repo + kas config you point it at -- and there is
# no built-in default: with all four of these left unset, 'setup' still does
# its whole job (volumes, kas-container, the shim, gitconfig) and just skips
# the project checkout step; 'smoketest' offers the meta-ai example below for
# one ephemeral run instead (never persisted, removed again afterward) if you
# run it with nothing configured at all.
#
# The values commented out below are the EXAMPLE, qualcomm-linux/meta-ai -- it
# is what has actually been tested end to end: the whole smoketest ladder
# (bitbake bash, tensorflow-lite, then llama-cpp) builds green on
# macOS/aarch64. To build your own layer set, set these four yourself;
# nothing else in mackas is project-specific.
#
# The repo mackas clones into work/${MACKAS_PROJECT_DIR}. For the meta-ai
# example, LAYERSERIES_COMPAT_ai="wrynose" and its LAYERDEPENDS are only core +
# openembedded-layer, so kas pulls openembedded-core + meta-openembedded/meta-oe
# + bitbake 2.18. No meta-qcom.
#MACKAS_PROJECT_URL="https://github.com/qualcomm-linux/meta-ai.git"
#MACKAS_PROJECT_BRANCH="wrynose"
#MACKAS_PROJECT_DIR="meta-ai"

# The kas files to compose, checkout-relative and colon-separated, exactly as
# kas-container wants them. mackas appends its generated kas/macos-local.yml
# after these, so do not list it yourself. This replaces the old MACKAS_MACHINE
# knob: name the machine config directly here.
#MACKAS_KAS_CONFIG="kas/base.yml:kas/qemuarm64.yml"

# Whether the env.sh 'kas-container' wrapper -- a bare, hand-typed
# 'kas-container build/shell/... <files>' -- also appends macos-local.yml onto
# <files> itself, the same way mackas's own subcommands already do. On by
# default; set to 0 to go back to composing it yourself (e.g. your own <files>
# already ends with a differently-named fragment).
#MACKAS_KAS_AUTO_FRAGMENT=1

# Whether that same wrapper also DERIVES the two settings above from the file
# list it was just given, and exports them into that shell: the first entry
# names the checkout under work/, so 'kas-container shell
# meta-angstrom/kas/angstrom.yml:meta-angstrom/kas/beaglebone.yml' yields
# MACKAS_PROJECT_DIR=meta-angstrom and
# MACKAS_KAS_CONFIG=kas/angstrom.yml:kas/beaglebone.yml. Without them,
# bitbake-getvar refuses and 'retrieve'/'buildstats analyze' either fail or
# silently use OE-core default paths a distro has redefined. On by default.
#
# Exported into that shell ONLY -- never written here, and never over a value
# already set. A chain spanning sibling layers derives nothing (there is no
# checkout-relative form of a sibling, since mackas cd's into one checkout).
#MACKAS_KAS_AUTO_PROJECT=1


# The smoketest ladder's build rungs: space-separated bitbake targets, run in
# order after the universal parse-only rung. Empty by default (the built-in
# default, not what is shown commented out below): with no targets set, the
# ladder's one build rung passes no --target at all, so kas/bitbake builds
# whatever default target the kas config itself names -- the same as running
# `kas build <config>` by hand with no target. Set your own project's targets
# to exercise more than that default, or use "bash" as a trivial fallback
# target if your kas config has no sensible bare default of its own.
#
# The line below is NOT the built-in default -- it is meta-ai's own ladder,
# kept here as a worked example of an override: smallest-first so failures
# stay cheap and specific (flatbuffers-tflite-native proves the toolchain,
# flatbuffers-tflite is the same recipe cross-compiled, then the real targets
# tensorflow-lite + llama-cpp, hours cold).
#MACKAS_SMOKETEST_TARGETS="flatbuffers-tflite-native flatbuffers-tflite tensorflow-lite llama-cpp"


# --- Host-side overhead sampling ---------------------------------------------

# `mackas smoketest` samples what each build rung costs the Mac itself -- host
# CPU-seconds and peak RSS of the Virtualization.framework VM plus the container
# daemons -- and appends it to the rung log. ON by default; it costs one `ps`
# per interval and never fails the build. Set to 0 to turn it off.
#MACKAS_OVERHEAD=1

# Seconds between samples. Sampling cannot see spikes shorter than this.
#MACKAS_OVERHEAD_INTERVAL=5
#
# Honest caveat: a fully-cached rung does almost no VM work, so its host figure
# is near zero -- that is correct, not a bug. And the guest-vs-host comparison
# needs the guest buildstats on the host first (`mackas retrieve buildstats`);
# until then the number is host cost, not isolated overhead. See docs/testing.md.

# Reclaim the sparse volume images with fstrim before and after every kas run,
# so they don't ratchet up build after build (a sparse ext4 image only grows;
# deleting files inside the guest never shrinks it -- see `mackas volume
# fstrim`). ON by default; fstrim is ~0.01s and runs when nothing holds the
# volumes, so it neither slows nor endangers the build. Set to 0 to turn it off.
# It must be exactly 0 or 1: any other value disables it (matching
# MACKAS_OVERHEAD -- only the literal "1" turns it on).
#MACKAS_FSTRIM_AUTO=1


# Clear tmp/buildstats in the TMPDIR volume before every kas run. ON by
# default: buildstats.bbclass opens build_stats in APPEND mode and never
# clears a BUILDNAME dir itself, so a project whose BUILDNAME does not vary
# per build (e.g. Angstrom's `BUILDNAME = "Angstrom ${DISTRO_VERSION}"`)
# would otherwise silently accumulate every build it has ever run into one
# build_stats file, inflating `mackas buildstats analyze`'s numbers. Set to 1
# to keep accumulating instead (you'll manage cleanup yourself -- `mackas
# retrieve buildstats` also warns and offers a one-off clear when it detects
# this). See docs/storage.md#buildstats-and-a-constant-buildname.
#MACKAS_BUILDSTATS_ACCUMULATE=0


# Live bitbake progress bridge (OPTIONAL, opt-in). With MACKAS_MONITOR=1 a build
# publishes its progress as JSON over HTTP from inside its own container, and
# `mackas monitor` polls it and prints one line per update. OFF by default: it
# bind-mounts an overlay, shadows the container's bitbake and runs a background
# HTTP thread on every build, so unlike MACKAS_OVERHEAD/MACKAS_FSTRIM_AUTO (which
# cost near nothing) it stays opt-in. See docs/architecture.md and README.
#MACKAS_MONITOR=0
# Host port the bridge publishes and `mackas monitor` polls.
#MACKAS_MONITOR_PORT=8801
# Have `mackas monitor` also post a native macOS notification on exactly three
# transitions -- build started, succeeded, failed -- never per task. Same as
# passing --notify. OFF by default: an unasked-for notification is worse than
# none. Real caveat, repeated in `mackas monitor --help`: with no Notification
# Center permission entry for your terminal app, osascript notifications are
# dropped SILENTLY -- no error, indistinguishable from working. Installing
# terminal-notifier avoids it (it carries its own bundle identity) and is
# preferred automatically when present.
#MACKAS_MONITOR_NOTIFY=0


# --- HTTP mirrors (OPTIONAL, opt-in) -----------------------------------------
#
# Mirrors of any kind are entirely optional. With MACKAS_USE_HTTP_MIRRORS=0 and
# MACKAS_USE_NFS_MIRRORS=0 -- both the default, i.e. doing nothing here -- mackas
# fetches everything from upstream and builds sstate locally, exactly like a
# stock kas-container checkout. Nothing below is needed to use mackas.
#
# If you DO want to reuse a cache you didn't build yourself, this is the
# recommended way to do it, over a mirror host's caches or your own:
#
# A Linux host that already has the NFS mount re-serves it read-only over HTTP
# (see mirror-server/mackas-mirrord), and the container reaches it over its
# vmnet NAT. No sudo, no NFS on the Mac, no virtiofs uncertainty -- and it is
# bitbake's own officially-supported mirror mechanism.
#
# mackas-mirrord also has a second, distinct use: run it ON THIS MAC pointed at
# your own local DL_DIR/SSTATE_DIR, and the container fetches them over HTTP
# instead of those directories being bind-mounted through virtiofs. Verified
# working (container -> host, both with and without auth) but its performance
# is NOT measured -- HTTP has per-request overhead a bind mount doesn't, so
# this is a trade-off for avoiding virtiofs specifically, not a free win. See
# "Serving local files instead of bind-mounting them" in docs/storage.md.
#
# Turning this on makes `setup` write the mirror stanza into the generated kas
# fragment, and `check` probe the mirror and report whether it answers.
#
# Mutually exclusive with MACKAS_USE_NFS_MIRRORS: both emit SSTATE_MIRRORS into
# the same fragment and bitbake's '?=' would make one silently win, so setting
# both is a hard error rather than a coin toss.
#MACKAS_USE_HTTP_MIRRORS=0

# Both default to port 8100 -- ONE mackas-mirrord process serving both caches
# under two path prefixes. 8100 is mackas-mirrord's own default port.
#
# These become, in the fragment:
#   SSTATE_MIRRORS ?= "file://.* ${MACKAS_HTTP_MIRROR_SSTATE}/PATH;downloadfilename=PATH"
#   SOURCE_MIRROR_URL ?= "${MACKAS_HTTP_MIRROR_DL}"
#
# 'PATH' is a literal placeholder bitbake substitutes with each object's hashed
# subpath, not a shell variable. ';downloadfilename=PATH' is valid (and needed)
# for http:// mirrors and NOT for the file:// ones further down.
#MACKAS_HTTP_MIRROR_SSTATE="http://linux-computer.local:8100/sstate"
#MACKAS_HTTP_MIRROR_DL="http://linux-computer.local:8100/downloads"
#
# If the mirror needs a password, do NOT put it in the URL. bitbake and wget
# read ~/.netrc, and kas-container forwards NETRC_FILE into the container:
#   printf 'machine linux-computer.local\n  login builder\n  password ...\n' > ~/.netrc
#   chmod 600 ~/.netrc
#   export NETRC_FILE=~/.netrc


# --- NFS mirrors (opt-in) ----------------------------------------------------

# A mirror host that exports e.g. /build/<build>/build/{downloads,sstate-cache}
# over NFS. Mounting them read-only as mirrors turns a cold multi-hour build
# into a mostly-sstate-hit one.
#
# Off by default: it needs sudo to mount, and a flaky NFS server turns every
# bitbake fetch into a hang. Turn it on once you've confirmed `check` is happy.
#MACKAS_USE_NFS_MIRRORS=0

#MACKAS_NFS_SERVER="linux-computer.local"
#MACKAS_NFS_EXPORT="/build"

# Where the export gets mounted. Defaults to $MACKAS_ROOT/nfs.
#MACKAS_NFS_MOUNT="$MACKAS_ROOT/nfs"

# Paths INSIDE the NFS mount. These get bind-mounted into the container as
# /sstate-mirror and /downloads-mirror (read-only) and referenced from the
# generated kas fragment.
#MACKAS_SSTATE_MIRROR_PATH="$MACKAS_NFS_MOUNT/angstrom-2026.06/build/sstate-cache"
#MACKAS_DL_MIRROR_PATH="$MACKAS_NFS_MOUNT/angstrom-2026.06/build/downloads"
#
# Note: macOS NFS needs sudo and almost always -o resvport:
#   sudo mount -t nfs -o resvport,vers=3,ro,nolocks,locallocks linux-computer.local:/build "$MACKAS_NFS_MOUNT"
#
# vers=3 is pinned ON PURPOSE. '-o vers=4' KERNEL-PANICS macOS (reproduced
# 2026-07-20 on 26.5.1 against a Linux nfs-kernel-server export) -- it takes
# the whole machine down, mid-build if you are unlucky. See docs/storage.md.


# --- Safety ------------------------------------------------------------------

# GiB of headroom `check` demands on top of the three volume caps added
# together before it will call the free-space check a PASS.
#MACKAS_FREE_SPACE_MARGIN_GB=20
